Activity 1, Link 3 (TutorialsPoint: E-mail Overview)
Describe: E-mail stands for electronic mail. Mail, either physical or electronic, has one purpose; to distribute information 
among people. E-mail, in contrast to its physical counterpart, is more "efficient" and "inexpensive." It's faster and cheaper 
than regular mail. Each user of an email is assigned a unique name for their account; this is commonly called an e-mail address. 
"Different users can send and receive messages according to the e-mail address." E-mail usually pertains to the form 
username@domain name. The username and the domain name are separated by the @ (at) symbol. E-mail addresses are not case-sensitive 
and spaces are not allowed in e-mail addresses. The components of an email itself, not address, are Header, Greeting, Text, and 
Signature. An E-mail Header is the first five lines of an E-mail message. The header part comprises the following fields:
From, is the sender's address, i.e. who sent the e-mail. Date, shows when the e-mail was sent. To, indication of the recipient's
address i.e. to whom the e-mail was sent. Subject, the purpose of the e-mail. CC, stands for Carbon copy. It "includes those 
recipient addresses whom we want to keep informed about but not exactly the intended recipient." BCC, stands for Black Carbon 
Copy, used when we do not want one or more of the recipients to know that someone else was copied on the message. 
Greeting is the opening of the actual message. Text represents the actual content of the message. Lastly, a Signature is the 
final part of an e-mail message. It includes Name of Sender, Address, and Contact Number. The page then lists out the 
advantages and disadvantages. The advantages aren't hard to understand. There are different means of convenience 
(ex: reliability, speed, inexpensive, etc.). However, the disadvantages are worth mentioning in depth: "Forgery, due to the 
sender usually not being authenticated in any way. Overloading and excessive e-mails may prevent accessibility. Junk emails, 
i.e., spam from unknown recipients can contribute to overload. Misdirection, due to it being possible that someone may send 
e-mails to the wrong person, or company and No Responses may be frustrating when the recipient does not read the e-mail and 
respond on a regular basis.
Learned: I have never heard of Carbon/ Black Carbon Copy in the context of an email.
Envision: Being more weary of senders without proper credentials.

Activity 3, Link 1 (Wikipedia: Mozilla Thunderbird)
Describe: Mozilla Thunderbird is a "personal information manager" that is primarily used as an" e-mail client" with calendar,
 contact book and news related functionalities. Thunderbird also features quick search, saved search folders, advanced message 
filtering, message grouping, and tags that help manage or find messages faster. As for junk filtering, Thunderbird incorporates
a Bayesian spam filter. In simplistic terms, Bayes are probabilistic classifiers, and in this case, that classifies spam 
together based on commonalities. Thunderbird also incorporates permit list and SpamAssassin, which are also spam identifiers.
Thunderbird uses many forms of standards support. POP and IMAP for basic email retrieval protocol. LDAP for address autocompletion. 
S/MIME and OpenPGP for email encryption support as well as, authentication support using OAuth. File Formats are supported using 
plugins "but this feature is not yet enabled due to related work in progress." "Thunderbird has integrated support for uploading 
files to online file storage services. Users can link large files instead of attaching them directly to the email message. 
Supported providers include Ubuntu One and Dropbox. Currently, file linking can be used through add-ons or by connecting to a 
WebDAV provider. "Thunderbird provides security features such as TLS/SSL connections to IMAP and SMTP servers. It also offers 
inbuilt support for secure email with digital signing and message encryption through OpenPGP (using public and private keys) or 
S/MIME (using certificates). Any of these security features can take advantage of smart cards with the installation of 
additional extensions." For security, "Thunderbird provides security features such as TLS/SSL connections to IMAP and SMTP 
servers. It also offers inbuilt support for secure email with digital signing and message encryption through OpenPGP 
(using public and private keys) or S/MIME (using certificates). Any of these security features can take advantage of smart cards 
with the installation of additional extensions."
Learned: I didn't anticipate how complicated email could be. It's also interesting to see statistics used in this context.
Envision: Using Thunderbird for better security and or convenience when needing to send emails with larger files.

Activity 4, Link 1 (Wikipedia: Email spoofing)
Describe: "Email spoofing is the creation of email messages with a forged sender address. The term applies to email purporting 
to be from an address which is not actually the sender's; mail sent in reply to that address may bounce or be delivered to an 
unrelated party whose identity has been faked. Disposable email address or "masked" email is a different topic, providing a 
masked email address that is not the user's normal address, which is not disclosed, but forwards mail sent to it to the user's 
real address."
Learned: I learned what a masked email is.
Envision: Be more weary online, in general.
